  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access / User Execution", "technique": "T1566.001 + T1204.002", "what_happened": "At 2026-11-02 01:48:32 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-DEV-12.corp.local (10.50.7.42): outlook.exe spawned winword.exe which opened the document 'Invoice_2024.docm' under user CORP\\svc-backup01. The .docm extension indicates a macro-enabled Word document — the canonical phishing-delivered initial-access vector. The opening time (01:48 in the morning, outside normal business hours for a workstation) and the user identity (a service-style account 'svc-backup01' opening an invoice document interactively) are both anomalous: a backup service account should not be interactively reading email attachments.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: parentImage=outlook.exe + image=winword.exe + commandLine contains 'Invoice_2024.docm' + user=CORP\\svc-backup01. Email-to-Word-macro is the classic Office macro-borne dropper chain.", "first_seen": "2026-11-02T01:48:32+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Execution / Command and Scripting Interpreter", "technique": "T1059.001 + T1059.003", "what_happened": "At 01:51:08 CEST (2 min 36 s after the .docm opened — typical macro auto-execution delay), Sysmon Event 1 fired: cmd.exe /c powershell.exe -nop -enc <base64> spawned from winword.exe on WS-DEV-12. The -nop -enc flags are textbook obfuscated PowerShell launcher behavior: -nop (NoProfile) bypasses user PS profile customizations that might log/inspect commands, -enc (EncodedCommand) feeds a base64-encoded payload that is NOT human-readable in the command line. Parent=winword.exe is the diagnostic feature — Word should never spawn powershell.exe in normal use. This is the macro payload executing.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: parentImage=winword.exe + image=cmd.exe + commandLine contains 'powershell.exe -nop -enc'. The Word→cmd→powershell parent chain plus the -enc flag is a MITRE T1059.001 PowerShell + T1059.003 cmd.exe combined signature, well-documented across macro-dropper campaigns.", "first_seen": "2026-11-02T01:51:08+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Masquerading (File Drop)", "technique": "T1036.005 + T1105", "what_happened": "At 01:52:18, Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) fired on WS-DEV-12: a binary 'C:\\Users\\Public\\update.exe' was created with signed=false and SHA256=a1b2c3d4e5f6071829304a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f6071829304a5b6c7d8e9. The drop location 'C:\\Users\\Public' is a writeable directory accessible to all user accounts and is a common choice for unsigned payloads — it is NOT a system path (so unsigned-binary policies that only check Program Files / Windows pass it) and it does NOT require admin rights. The filename 'update.exe' is masquerading-by-name: 'update' connotes legitimate Microsoft / vendor update activity, lowering analyst suspicion at a glance. The fact that signed=false combined with the Public-folder drop and the generic-update filename is a triple-red-flag combination.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11: targetFilename=C:\\Users\\Public\\update.exe + signed=false + hashes=SHA256=a1b2c3d4e5f6071829304a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f6071829304a5b6c7d8e9. T1036.005 (match-legitimate-name masquerading) + T1105 (ingress tool transfer — the binary content arrived via the macro PowerShell).", "first_seen": "2026-11-02T01:52:18+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence / Registry Run Key", "technique": "T1547.001", "what_happened": "At 01:52:19 (1 second after the binary drop), Sysmon Event 13 (RegistryValueSet) fired on WS-DEV-12: targetObject=HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\\UpdaterSvc with details=C:\\Users\\Public\\update.exe. This is the canonical T1547.001 Registry Run Key persistence touch: any value placed under HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run executes automatically every time the user logs on. HKCU (HKEY_CURRENT_USER) does NOT require admin rights to write — the service-account user CORP\\svc-backup01 has full rights to its own HKCU hive. The value name 'UpdaterSvc' continues the masquerading-as-legitimate-update naming theme started by 'update.exe'. The 1-second gap between binary drop and Run-key write is diagnostic of an automated installer (no human types two filesystem and registry operations 1 second apart).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 13: targetObject contains the literal path 'HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\\UpdaterSvc' + details points to the just-dropped update.exe. Classic T1547.001 Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder.", "first_seen": "2026-11-02T01:52:19+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Command and Control (Initial Beacon)", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1571", "what_happened": "At 01:52:25 (6 seconds after the Run-key write), Sysmon Event 1 fired: update.exe launched for the first time directly (parent=cmd.exe from the same PowerShell chain — the macro chose to launch its dropped binary immediately rather than wait for the next logon). Simultaneously, Sysmon Event 3 (NetworkConnect) fired: outbound TCP connection from update.exe (PID matched) to 45.95.169.142:443. Port 443 is HTTPS — chosen for L7 firewall blendiness. The destination 45.95.169.142 is a public IP not associated with any business partner, CDN, or Microsoft endpoint. This is the initial C&C beacon — the attacker's foothold callback to confirm the implant is alive and request further instructions.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=C:\\Users\\Public\\update.exe + parentImage=cmd.exe + user=CORP\\svc-backup01. Sysmon Event 3: image=C:\\Users\\Public\\update.exe + destinationIp=45.95.169.142 + destinationPort=443. The bind from process to network is via the same Sysmon ProcessGuid.", "first_seen": "2026-11-02T01:52:25+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Persistence Validated / Autostart Triggered at Logon", "technique": "T1547.001", "what_happened": "The next day at 08:14:02 (user login time during normal business hours), Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-DEV-12: update.exe launched with parentImage=explorer.exe. The explorer.exe parent (the Windows shell, which processes the HKCU Run keys at user logon) is the diagnostic fingerprint of an HKCU-Run-key-triggered launch. This is NOT the same as the 01:52:25 first launch (parent was cmd.exe from the macro chain) — this is the PERSISTENCE working as intended: every time CORP\\svc-backup01 logs in, update.exe will be re-executed automatically. The attacker has achieved durable foothold survival across reboots and re-logons without needing to maintain access by other means.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=C:\\Users\\Public\\update.exe + parentImage=explorer.exe + user=CORP\\svc-backup01 + @timestamp at logon time. The explorer.exe-launches-Run-key-binary-at-logon pattern is the canonical T1547.001 persistence-firing signature.", "first_seen": "2026-11-02T08:14:02+0100"}
